# M24.2b -- run RTEMS's own `hello` sample, built for zynqmp_rpu_lock_step with the
# container-built toolchain (third_party/rtems-container/output), on Renode's mainline Zynq
# UltraScale+ Cortex-R5 platform (docs/open-questions.md question 144), on rpu0 only
# (lock-step BSP; apu*/rpu1 left halted). Adapted from the read-only host reference
# third_party/rtems/run-renode/hello.resc (only the source ELF differs; the platform/reset
# convention is identical to that script and to the M24.1 spike's setup.resc). UART0 output
# is captured to a file via `CreateFileBackend` (headless-safe; `showAnalyzer` needs a GUI
# window and is not used here).
#
# `hello` explicitly disables the clock driver (testsuites/samples/hello/init.c:
# CONFIGURE_APPLICATION_DOES_NOT_NEED_CLOCK_DRIVER) and only prints a few lines once, so a
# short virtual run is ample. Expected output stated in REPORT.md before running (guessed from
# the sample's name/purpose):
#   *** HELLO WORLD TEST ***
#   Hello World
#   *** END OF HELLO WORLD TEST ***
# ACTUAL captured output differs in exact banner text (RTEMS 6's own test-banner macros use
# "BEGIN OF TEST <name>"/"END OF TEST <name>", not "<NAME> TEST"/"END OF <NAME> TEST", and the
# run also prints an RTEMS version/shutdown banner the guess did not anticipate) -- see
# REPORT.md's "Renode runs" section for the real, captured text. Recorded as a finding, not
# silently corrected in the comment to look like the guess was right.

:name: zynqmp-rpu-lockstep-hello

using sysbus
using sysbus.cluster0
using sysbus.cluster1

mach create "zynqmp-rpu-hello"

machine LoadPlatformDescription @platforms/cpus/zynqmp.repl

# NOTE (bug found and fixed in this task, see REPORT.md): `@$ORIGIN/...` combines the `@`
# literal-path-token marker with the `$ORIGIN` variable -- `@` makes the parser take the
# whole following text as a literal string, which suppresses `$ORIGIN` substitution, so
# Renode tried to create a file in a literal directory named `$ORIGIN` and failed ("File
# $ORIGIN/hello_uart.log could not be created"), aborting the rest of this script before
# `quit` -- this, not the `start`/`RunFor` ordering above, was the actual cause of the
# hang (confirmed with a raw byte-level dump of the monitor's response). `LoadELF
# $ORIGIN/hello.exe` below has no `@` and substitutes correctly; the fix here is the same:
# drop the `@` so `$ORIGIN` is substituted before the path is used.
uart0 CreateFileBackend $ORIGIN/hello_uart.log true

macro reset
"""
    cluster0 ForEach IsHalted true
    cluster1 ForEach IsHalted true
    rpu0 IsHalted false

    sysbus LoadELF $ORIGIN/hello.exe cpu=rpu0
"""

runMacro $reset

# NOTE (bug found and fixed in this task, see REPORT.md): do NOT call `start` before
# `emulation RunFor` -- `start` puts the emulation into continuous free-run mode, and RunFor
# then fails ("This action is not available when emulation is already started"). Because
# that error aborts the rest of an `include`d/CLI-run script before reaching `quit`, Renode
# is left idling at the monitor prompt forever with no further input coming -- this is
# exactly the hang this task hit first (5m46s wall, ~5s CPU, zero UART output, process never
# exited). `RunFor` starts the (paused, freshly-reset) emulation itself; confirmed against
# the M24.1 spike's own working driver (measure_virtual_time.py), which never calls `start`
# either and reliably measured 0 ns drift over 1000 RunFor steps.
# RunFor duration reduced from the original 2s (see REPORT.md "Renode wall-clock cost is far
# from real-time for the full BSP/platform, not the M24.1 spike's bare-loop firmware" --
# RunFor "2" alone exceeded a 120s real-time budget and had to be killed mid-run, even though
# the UART output shows the guest program had already reached "[ RTEMS shutdown ]" long
# before that: the platform keeps burning real wall time simulating idle virtual time after
# the guest program's own work is done, and that idle-simulation cost is what dominates, not
# the actual program logic). `hello` disables the clock driver and finishes almost instantly
# in virtual time, so 0.3s of virtual runtime is ample margin.
emulation RunFor "0.3"
quit
